/**

@page tutorials_exahype2_hpc_offline-load-balancing Offline load balancing

@tableofcontents

Load balancing is notoriously hard and devising a bespoke load balancing for 
your specific application can be time consuming and challenging. ExaHyPE comes
along with a set of generic load balancing strategies, but, due to the fact 
that they are so generic, they often struggle to get the performance right 
unless developers invest a significant amount of effort.

One straightforward starting point to get reasonable load balancing is to 
assume that a mesh were static and to work with some offloading load balancing
algorithm. That is, we make all load balancing decisions ahead of compile time,
and then hard-code the load balancing. Indeed, this might even provide the 
baseline for something more bespoke, generic and dynamic.

The following discussion illustrates the ideas behind offline load balancing
for the single black hole benchmark of the @ref applications_exahype2_ExaGRyPE ExaGRyPE
solver. Actually, we use a slimmed down version of one of its standard benchmarks.
However, the offline load balancing is not specific to ExaGRyPE: It works with
any ExaHyPE application (and also with other Peano-based codes, even though they
might not have a built-in support yet).

For a complete reference of the offline decomposition interfaces and staged splitting workflow,
see @ref page_toolbox_loadbalancing_api.


# Prepare the benchmark

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/BH_LMC.png" width="60%"/>


We follow more or less the instructions on @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole.
The page provides more in-depth information on which modules you need, what 
other tools you might want to combine with ExaHyPE, and so forth. 
For the present tutorial, we assume that you open a terminal window in Peano's 
tutorials directory tutorials/exahype2/hpc.
After setting the correct environment variables

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=~/git/Peano/python:~/git/Peano/src:~/git/Peano/applications/exahype2/ccz4:~/git/Peano/benchmarks/exahype2/ccz4/single-black-hole
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where you replace the ```~/git/Peano``` with the right path on your particular 
system, a simple

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py --trees 27 --cell-size 1.8 --load-balancing-metric offline-regular-fine-grid
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

should build your setup. If you favour the big run, where all of the features 
that we discuss below are already in, that would be somehow equal to  

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py -s fd4-rk1-ps-3 -et 1e-2 --plot 1e-2 --scheduler no-tasks --trees 27 --cell-size 1.8 --output test --load-balancing-metric offline-regular
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~



# Studying the Python code

All the work setting up the benchmark is in tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py.
Here we choose the load balancing strategy that we want to use along with many other benchmark parameters.
Some of the more specific details are in tutorial_single_black_hole_settings.py, in particular 
add_hardcoded_load_balancing_over_regular_mesh(...), which shows how to use the hardcoded load balancer.
This tutorial focuses on a concrete workflow; the full API contracts and design rationale are
documented in @ref page_toolbox_loadbalancing_api.
When we do a first sanity check and compile with 


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py ... --load-balancing-metric none
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

we see that this means

@snippet tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py tutorials_exahype2_hpc_tutorial_offline_load_balancing_none

so no load balancing at all is happening. We can run this code and it should 
give us a single tree without any decomposition. You can visualise it in 
Paraview.

# Offload load balancing for a regular grid

## Fine grid mesh association

The simplest way to load balance offline for a regular grid - and a good 
starting point prior to doing something interesting - is the following idea:

- Tell the offline load balancer that there will be a regular mesh. Such a mesh
  can be synthesised and we don't have to mess around with some complex AMR
  structure.
- Tell the offline load balancer to partition only the finest mesh.
- Feed that mesh back into a hard-coded partitioning and let ExaGRyPE run.

The resulting mesh should be optimal as the load balancer can distribute all 
the leaves equally, and should yield reasonable speedup as ExaGRyPE does all
of its calculations in the leaves, even though it will have severe 
shortcomings.

### Import correct packages

The first step is to import the offline load balancing properly:

@snippet tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py tutorials_exahype2_hpc_tutorial_offline_load_balancing_inputs

### Create a regular mesh representation for the offline load balancing

After that, we can create a representation for the library:

@snippet tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py tutorials_exahype2_hpc_tutorial_offline_load_balancing_regular_fine_grid

The print statement above should yield an output similar to

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 tutorial_offline_load_balancing_sbh.py --trees 27 --cell-size 1.8 --load-balancing-metric offline-regular-fine-grid

[...]

Generating 3D regular grid 'pytest-hardcoded-lb-3d' with 3 levels
  Level 1: 3^3 = 27 cells
  Level 2: 9^3 = 729 cells
  Level 3: 27^3 = 19,683 cells
Total cells generated: 20,439
Regular grid generation completed successfully
  Tree: pytest-hardcoded-lb-3d
  Leaf count: 19683
  Subtree count: 1
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

which tells us that we have indeed created one big tree with three levels. 



